n/math-academy-id "q-141082" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9eb1ace-da7e-5399-a3fe-3eca3341dc04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437950} {:answer/id #uuid "8294de83-bfe6-5d3e-907f-46fca51b308f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9eb1ace-da7e-5399-a3fe-3eca3341dc04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437950} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f826ec9-1240-515d-9e72-2dd14068a0b0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439004} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "307652cb-8dec-5139-b1b9-8b42d2c243ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437952} {:answer/id #uuid "d0fc6fa1-75d1-54ae-a895-4bd473e2976a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "307652cb-8dec-5139-b1b9-8b42d2c243ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437952} :answer-field/id #uuid "93d26810-7687-5dab-8301-97c0074dc993" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439005} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "430fce2c-320b-5e26-93df-7d20527603a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437954} {:answer/id #uuid "4dee6609-1868-55be-9c34-9e4174a83b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "430fce2c-320b-5e26-93df-7d20527603a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437954} :answer-field/id #uuid "81855480-1021-5030-8cb7-a5a2db6e6e14" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4402732-29b4-541b-a6e6-a372fd33bf97" :question/math-academy-id "q-141084" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & \\ne -\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\ne -\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "654fa534-773a-593f-977c-52c967bc6758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437956} {:answer/id #uuid "3e1670b2-e9d2-5154-a206-4f8b1150ce6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "654fa534-773a-593f-977c-52c967bc6758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437956} :answer-field/id #uuid "3757fe80-c2f2-5c86-bda7-b8b1794c555f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439007} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7283afb-092e-5737-a0c8-37560698d7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437958} {:answer/id #uuid "23367094-d892-5aa7-a778-191ce481dae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7283afb-092e-5737-a0c8-37560698d7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437958} :answer-field/id #uuid "517f6faa-1205-51b0-a3ed-acee4119eb37" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439008} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80cf2045-8273-57f1-bbce-39810be44be4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437960} {:answer/id #uuid "814d7525-ee34-5bf5-a23b-7ddd661f2801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80cf2045-8273-57f1-bbce-39810be44be4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437960} :answer-field/id #uuid "da8265e0-a0fc-5f6a-becf-ca88b952fcad" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33a5a8c7-40f0-5353-a590-88087e77753a" :question/math-academy-id "q-141086" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=3{e}^{-x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{-x}$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=3{e}^{-x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(3{e}^{-x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne 3{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=3{e}^{-x}$ and ${y}^{′}=-3{e}^{-x}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-3y \\\\ -3{e}^{-x} & \\overset{?}{=}-3(3{e}^{-x}) \\\\ -3{e}^{-x} & \\ne -9{e}^{-x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=3{e}^{-x}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce779982-5956-53ce-8f93-98847edcb147" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437962} {:answer/id #uuid "20333a47-4e71-5ec5-9d41-91782cb4b909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce779982-5956-53ce-8f93-98847edcb147" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437962} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cefe8de-ad3c-5341-b5a8-44e7ce7436d0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439010} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4113f3b5-d089-56b5-ba2d-bdb03486c6dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437964} {:answer/id #uuid "1edc75ea-b6e2-5bab-aebd-7ef159ff5c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1edc75ea-b6e2-5bab-aebd-7ef159ff5c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437965} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1cc3bda-6bd3-5d8e-bcf2-7853efc526c4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439011} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ca0f560-582b-5ed2-ae44-d30f3b653470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437966} {:answer/id #uuid "0a6fcbcf-baa0-52ca-98cd-0ff0fd8d97f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a6fcbcf-baa0-52ca-98cd-0ff0fd8d97f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437967} :answer-field/id #uuid "26f1c7da-9254-5126-a28b-3e9af246aaa4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c8904e1-5f17-5951-8dbc-61b76cd0babc" :question/math-academy-id "q-141088" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{4x}{{e}^{y/2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4x}$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x} \\\\  & \\ne \\frac{1}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{4}{x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & =\\frac{4}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3562e5cf-f895-5f46-86d8-1f4d377e852c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437968} {:answer/id #uuid "54204ebf-cf84-5ca0-9a31-05ad473701a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3562e5cf-f895-5f46-86d8-1f4d377e852c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437968} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b4d80e4-40c9-5772-9f3f-8334efd87237" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439013} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47f8cc69-64ad-5f3d-990f-9498a8c363e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437970} {:answer/id #uuid "2c072334-a0f1-5100-a69e-a1a0ab21bc24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c072334-a0f1-5100-a69e-a1a0ab21bc24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437971} :answer-field/id #uuid "275a3437-057b-583e-8933-7c02f90a39d2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439014} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ca6983f-28cb-5d2b-9d76-a0663297c3a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437972} {:answer/id #uuid "a63eedbc-25e0-50eb-944b-f8fe7cfea503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a63eedbc-25e0-50eb-944b-f8fe7cfea503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437973} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce33855e-6605-5dff-989b-b31182ec13fc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8806c061-892c-5fd2-baf1-cd903aa5ea31" :question/math-academy-id "q-141089" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{′}(x)=-5x$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-6x \\\\  & \\ne -5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-3{x}^{2}$ and ${y}^{′}=-6x$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -6x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2(-3{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ -6x & =-6x\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-3{x}^{2}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}]}
